On 2008-03-09, John H. <JohnWH@gmail.com> wrote:
> As for crowning, I am not siding with the others in saying it
> shouldn't be made more useful.  The idea that it is intended to be a
> negative benefit, I think, is an assumed attitude based off of a
> statement in a spoiler and the present game effect.  

I don't think anyone believes that crowning is a _negative_ benefit;
it's just that what it provides is generally considered to be something
the player has likely already obtained elsewhere, and then provides a
downside on top of that.

I think people perceive the intent to be god's way of saying "Here's a
lot of stuff; if this isn't enough to get the Amulet, you're beyond
help, so quit bugging me so much."

> Crowning is one of the coolest ideas in Nethack: it gives the player 
> a title, affects shopkeeper greetings, and gets noted in the Final 
> Attributes list.  

All this is window dressing, though, and irrelevant to gameplay.

> I don't think it should be dismissed as mostly a way to make prayer
> harder.  

It's both a way to make prayer harder AND a quick way to get
disintegration resistance.  Oh, and if you're just slung-up dead-nuts
hopeless, you can get a particular weapon artifact too, or the Finger of
Death spellbook as a wiz.

In practice, there's not usually much harm in being crowned for an
experienced player, because he's not praying that often anyway and
Gehennom is "out of bounds" for such a thing to begin with.  But there
also isn't typically much benefit unless he really wants that finger of
death -- disintegration resistance is already covered by vanilla's
reflection requirement. 

Lawfuls already have Excalibur.  Chaotics probably got Stormy long ago
during the whole sac-festing process leading up to the crowning.

Neutrals may or may not have had Vorpy, but Vorpy is the weakest of the
above three artifacts anyway, so it's only good if, again, you're just
in a dead-nuts hopeless slot as far as weapons go.

